/-!
# Finiteness of a Noetherian normalization over a complete pair

Source: Marot, Sur les anneaux universellement japonais (1975), Proposition 2.1,
printed p.106, applied as in Proposition 2.3, printed p.107; Stacks Tag 0334
(blueprint: lem-per-adic-noetherian-normalization-finite).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

namespace integralClosure

/-- A Noetherian integral closure in a finite fraction-field extension is finite over
an adically complete Noetherian domain with Nagata closed fibre.

Source: Marot (1975), Proposition 2.1, printed p.106; Nagata implies universally
Japanese by Stacks Tag 0334
(blueprint: lem-per-adic-noetherian-normalization-finite).

Proof:
1. The closure T is integral over R [def-weil-pbc-integral-closure]. Completeness
   puts J in the Jacobson radical [lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical];
   integrality puts JT in the Jacobson radical of T
   [lem-weil-pbc-integral-algebra-ideal-in-radical]. Krull intersection over the
   Noetherian ring T [lem-weil-pbc-krull-intersection-jacobson] gives intersection
   of J^n T equal to zero. Thus T is separated and JT is proper.
2. The nonzero quotient T/JT is Noetherian
   [lem-weil-pbc-surjective-image-noetherian] and has finitely many minimal primes
   [lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-finitely-many-minimal-primes]. Every prime contains a
   minimal prime: apply Zorn to primes below it, using that the intersection of a
   chain of primes is prime. Lift these minimal primes to P_i in T. The radical
   r of JT is their intersection [lem-weil-pbc-zero-locus-eq-iff-radical-eq], and
   is finitely generated by Noetherianity [def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring].
3. Contract P_i to p_i in R. Since p_i contains J, Nagata R/J makes R/p_i
   Japanese [def-weil-pbc-nagata-ring]. The prime-quotient normalization lemma
   [lem-per-normalization-japanese-prime-quotient-finite] makes T/P_i finite over
   R/p_i, hence over R by lifting coefficients.
4. The diagonal T/r into the finite product of T/P_i is injective because its
   kernel is the intersection of the P_i modulo r. Coordinatewise generators
   make this product finite over R; the Noetherian submodule theorem
   [lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-submodule-finite] makes T/r finite over R.
5. Degree-n monomials in finite ideal generators of r generate r^n/r^(n+1)
   over T/r, hence over R. Lifting generators across the successive quotient
   sequences proves inductively that every T/r^n, n positive, is finite over R.
   This includes r = 0 with its empty family of ideal generators.
6. Since r is finitely generated and is the radical of JT, a positive power
   r^t lies in JT [lem-weil-pbc-fg-ideal-radical-power]. Thus T/JT is a quotient
   of the finite module T/r^t; reducing coefficients makes it finite over R/J.
7. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-finite-over-complete-ring] (Stacks Tag 031D) to the
   complete ring R, the J-adically separated module T from step 1, and its finite
   reduction from step 6. It gives that T is finite over R, without assuming T
   complete, R normal or local, or L/K separable. -/
theorem finite_of_noetherian_of_adicComplete
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra R K] [IsFractionRing R K]
    (J : Ideal R) (_hJ : J ≠ ⊤) [IsAdicComplete J R]
    (_hNagata : IsNagataRing (R ⧸ J))
    (L : Type w) [Field L] [Algebra K L] [Algebra R L] [IsScalarTower R K L]
    [FiniteDimensional K L] [IsNoetherianRing (integralClosure R L)] :
    Module.Finite R (integralClosure R L) := by
  sorry

end integralClosure
